    "text": "**Case A**\n\n- **A1 \u2014 Yes.** All earlier requests came from one region with a stable fingerprint, and the seven-day search, which covers the whole period since publication, found nothing else. This assumes the key was not leaked by another route more than seven days ago.\n- **A2 \u2014 Yes.** The burst happened at 19 minutes and the check scored the account at 30 minutes, so the new proxy, new fingerprint and payout changes already existed when it ran and it did not flag them. This assumes the check had access to request and account-change data, which the case does not state.\n- **A3 \u2014 No, not by itself.** A shared payout destination fits one owner, but it equally fits a shared processor, an agent, or a mule account used by several parties.\n- **A4 \u2014 Yes, weakly.** A diverted payout would normally prompt a complaint from the account owner within 30 days, so the silence counts for something. It is only weak evidence because reports can lag or never be filed.\n- **A5 \u2014 Yes.** A new network path and fingerprint 19 minutes after publication, combined with a payout destination change and a faster schedule, is the classic takeover pattern.\n\n**Case B**\n\n- **B1 \u2014 Yes.** Each account has 100 transactions, so the rates are directly comparable: D is 0 percent against 2 percent for the others. The gap is small in absolute terms (two chargebacks), so it says little about underlying risk.\n- **B2 \u2014 No.** A combined figure is an average across the four accounts, so one account can sit above 5 percent while others sit below.\n- **B3 \u2014 Yes.** The search covered all merchant accounts and found only A and the closed account. It shows nothing about accounts held under other names, which is the very thing the one-operation hypothesis alleges for B to D.\n- **B4 \u2014 Cannot tell.** Different analysts on different days is not enough; we are not told whether they worked from the same evidence or whether the second had seen the first no